-      "id": "US-996",
-      "title": "LLM provider latency SLO tracker with automatic provider demotion",
-      "priority": "medium",
-      "description": "The multi-provider fallback chain in settings.json is statically ordered by priority. In practice, a provider can enter a degraded state where it responds slowly (> 3 s) without triggering a full circuit-breaker trip. The current circuit-breaker only opens on hard errors. A latency SLO tracker should monitor the rolling P95 latency per provider and automatically demote slow providers to a lower priority in the live ordering, restoring them once latency normalises. This prevents the common scenario where a sluggish primary provider degrades every response before the circuit-breaker trips.",
+      "id": "US-991",
+      "title": "Inbound webhook idempotency guard using message-ID deduplication",
+      "priority": "high",
+      "description": "WhatsApp Cloud API delivers webhooks at-least-once; duplicate message deliveries are a documented normal operating condition, not an edge case. The current BullMQ pipeline does not perform explicit deduplication keyed on the WhatsApp message ID, so a retried webhook can produce duplicate AI responses sent to the same guest. An idempotency guard using the message ID as a Redis SETNX key (with a short TTL) must be inserted at the top of the webhook ingestion path before enqueuing.",
       "acceptanceCriteria": [
-        "Each AI provider call records response latency; a rolling P95 window (configurable, default last 20 calls) is maintained per provider",
-        "When a provider's P95 exceeds a configurable slow_threshold_ms (default 3000 ms), it is temporarily demoted below all providers that are currently within SLO",
-        "Provider is restored to its configured priority once P95 drops below slow_threshold_ms for a configurable recovery window (default 5 consecutive calls)",
-        "Current provider priority order (including any demotions) is visible in the admin AI providers panel",
-        "A Vitest property test asserts that a simulated slow provider is always ranked below a fast provider after the demotion threshold is crossed"
+        "When the same WhatsApp message ID arrives twice within 24 hours, the second delivery is ACK'd with HTTP 200 but not enqueued",
+        "Deduplication key is the messages[].id field from the Cloud API payload",
+        "Redis key TTL is configurable (default 24 h) and documented in settings.json",
+        "A Vitest test simulates two identical payloads and asserts only one queue job is created",
+        "Deduplication skip events are logged with the duplicate message ID for debugging"
       ],
       "technicalNotes": [
-        "Demotion state should be in-memory (not DB) and reset on process restart to avoid persistent incorrectly-demoted providers",
-        "Integrate with existing circuit-breaker.ts and llm-settings-loader.ts; the active provider list emitted by the loader should reflect real-time demotion",
-        "OTEL AI agent observability articles recommend latency-based routing as a key 2025 reliability pattern (https://opentelemetry.io/blog/2025/ai-agent-observability/)"
+        "Use ioredis SETNX + EXPIRE or SET NX EX for atomic check-and-set",
+        "WhatsApp retries for up to 7 days with exponential backoff on non-200 responses (https://hookdeck.com/webhooks/platforms/guide-to-whatsapp-webhooks-features-and-best-practices)",
+        "Existing BullMQ queue in message_queue config is the right insertion point; add a pre-enqueue guard"
