+      "id": "US-1006",
+      "title": "HTTP security headers hardening with Helmet.js configuration audit",
+      "priority": "low",
+      "description": "The Express server serves both the API and the webchat widget static files. A 2025 OWASP Node.js hardening audit would flag missing or misconfigured HTTP security headers as a common vulnerability. Specifically, the Permissions-Policy header (blocking microphone/camera/geolocation access), the X-Content-Type-Options header (preventing MIME sniffing), and a strict Referrer-Policy should be verified. Helmet.js is already a standard recommendation for Express apps; this story ensures all recommended Helmet modules are enabled and correctly tuned for Rainbow's route structure.",
+      "acceptanceCriteria": [
+        "All Express routes respond with: X-Content-Type-Options: nosniff, X-Frame-Options: DENY, Referrer-Policy: strict-origin-when-cross-origin, and Permissions-Policy: microphone=(), camera=(), geolocation=()",
+        "Content-Security-Policy is tightened to block inline scripts on admin UI pages (hash-based nonces acceptable for dynamic scripts)",
+        "An automated test (using supertest) asserts the presence and correct value of each required header on the /health, /admin, and /webchat routes",
+        "The existing CSP connect-src test suite continues to pass after the Helmet config change",
+        "No existing functionality (webchat widget, SSE streaming, webhook ingestion) is broken by the new headers"
+      ],
+      "technicalNotes": [
+        "OWASP Node.js Cheat Sheet recommends Helmet with all modules enabled as a baseline (https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Nodejs_Security_Cheat_Sheet.html)",
+        "Helmet v7+ includes helmet.permissionsPolicy() and sensible defaults; review each module against the existing CSP connect-src whitelist",
+        "SSE (Server-Sent Events) streaming for AI responses requires specific Cache-Control headers that must not be overridden by Helmet defaults"
+      ],
